VERIFIED FACTS (from a prior survey; do not re-derive)
Repo is `p-queue`. `PQueue` class in `source/index.ts` starts line 97. `PriorityQueue` default in `source/priority-queue.ts` (sorted array, NOT a heap, no id map), `#findIndex(predicate)` is its id lookup. `#queue` field in PQueue is `QueueType = Queue<RunFunction, QueueAddOptions>` at `source/index.ts` line 128. `#waitingTasks` is `Set<WaitingTask>` at line 154; `WaitingTask` has optional `id?` and `priority?`. Running tasks are removed from `#waitingTasks` before running and are dequeued from `#queue`, so a scan of `#waitingTasks` by id is how PQueue distinguishes a waiting id from an unknown or already-running id.

`source/queue.ts` current full text:
```ts
export type RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>;

export type Queue<Element, Options> = {
	size: number;
	filter: (options: Readonly<Partial<Options>>) => Element[];
	dequeue: () => Element | undefined;
	enqueue: (run: Element, options?: Partial<Options>) => void;
	setPriority: (id: string, priority: number) => void;
	remove: (id: string) => void;
};
```

`source/index.ts` current `setPriority` (lines 837-852):
```ts
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
        throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
    }

    // Checked here, not only in `PriorityQueue`, so that it also throws with a custom queue class.
    for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
        if (waitingTask.id === id) {
            this.#queue.setPriority(id, priority);
            waitingTask.priority = priority;
            return;
        }
    }

    throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
}
```

`source/priority-queue.ts` current `setPriority` (lines 92-105):
```ts
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    const index = this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id);
    if (index === -1) {
        throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
    }

    // Moving it would put it behind the tasks with the same priority.
    if (this.#queue[index]!.priority === priority) {
        return;
    }

    const [item] = this.#queue.splice(index, 1);
    this.enqueue(item!.run, {priority, id});
}
```

EXACT ERROR STRINGS (reuse these everywhere):
- TypeError: `` `Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})` ``
- ReferenceError: `` `No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.` ``

IMPLEMENTATION

1. `source/queue.ts` — add this OPTIONAL member after `setPriority` (keep `setPriority` required):
```ts
	setPriorities?: (updates: ReadonlyArray<{id: string; priority: number}>) => void;
```

2. `source/priority-queue.ts` — add `setPriorities` after `setPriority`. It must validate the ENTIRE batch before any mutation, then reuse single-update ordering by applying `this.setPriority` in order. Use exactly this logic:
```ts
setPriorities(updates: ReadonlyArray<{id: string; priority: number}>) {
    for (const {id, priority} of updates) {
        if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
            throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
        }

        if (this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id) === -1) {
            throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
        }
    }

