timeline.detail:
what
Add a bulk `setPriorities` method to `PQueue`. The repository is the `p-queue` source, with the public API in `source/index.ts`, the queue abstraction in `source/queue.ts`, and the default queue implementation in `source/priority-queue.ts`. There is no existing `setPriorities`, no `_setPriority` private helper, and no `benchmark_tests/` directory.

paths
- PQueue implementation: `source/index.ts` (class starts line 97; `setPriority` lines 837-852)
- Queue abstraction: `source/queue.ts` (10 lines total)
- Default priority queue: `source/priority-queue.ts` (140 lines total; `setPriority` lines 92-105)
- Lower-bound insertion helper: `source/lower-bound.ts`
- Options types: `source/options.ts`
- Existing PQueue priority-ordering tests: `test/basic.ts`, `test/advanced.ts`, `test/validation.ts`, `test/debug.ts`
- Existing PriorityQueue unit tests: `test/priority-queue.ts`
- Test utilities (including `FifoQueue` custom queue): `test/helpers.ts`
- Type-level fixtures: `test/fixtures/types.ts`; runner `test/types.ts`
- Benchmarks: `bench.ts`
- Package config: `package.json`; TS config: `tsconfig.json`

facts

1. Existing single-update ordering method in `PQueue`

`source/index.ts` lines 837-852, exact:

```ts
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
        throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
    }

    // Checked here, not only in `PriorityQueue`, so that it also throws with a custom queue class.
    for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
        if (waitingTask.id === id) {
            this.#queue.setPriority(id, priority);
            waitingTask.priority = priority;
            return;
        }
    }

    throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
}
```

JSDoc for this method is lines 802-836. There is no `_setPriority` helper. Validation is inline, not a separate priority helper. Other top-level validation helpers exist nearby: `validateTimeout` (lines 45-49), `validateLimit` (lines 53-57), `validateRateLimit` (lines 59-78).

2. `Queue` interface

`source/queue.ts`, full current text:

```ts
export type RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>;

export type Queue<Element, Options> = {
	size: number;
	filter: (options: Readonly<Partial<Options>>) => Element[];
	dequeue: () => Element | undefined;
	enqueue: (run: Element, options?: Partial<Options>) => void;
	setPriority: (id: string, priority: number) => void;
	remove: (id: string) => void;
};
```

`setPriority` is currently a required member. Any new `setPriorities` added as required would break `Queue` implementers in tests/docs (`test/helpers.ts` `FifoQueue`, `test/fixtures/types.ts` `FifoQueue` and `GroupQueue`, readme custom queue). Making it optional avoids that.

3. `PriorityQueue` implementation

`source/priority-queue.ts` lines 1-140. It implements `Queue<RunFunction, PriorityQueueOptions>`. Data structure is a sorted array with `undefined` consumed slots and a `#head` cursor; it is NOT a heap and there is NO `Map<id, node>`. Important declarations:

```ts
export type PriorityQueueOptions = {
	priority?: number | undefined;
} & QueueAddOptions;

export default class PriorityQueue implements Queue<RunFunction, PriorityQueueOptions> {
	// The slots before `#head` are `undefined`.
	readonly #queue: Array<PriorityQueueOptions & {run: RunFunction} | undefined> = [];

	// The queue is stored as a sorted array, but dequeued slots are left before `#head` until compaction. Only items from `#head` onward are live, which keeps repeated dequeues amortized O(1).
	#head = 0;
```

`#compact()` lines 18-26, `#findIndex` lines 29-37, `#removeFirst` lines 40-56. `enqueue` lines 58-90 uses `lowerBound` for binary insertion and appends when the tail priority is >= new priority. Current `setPriority` lines 92-105, exact:

```ts
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    const index = this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id);
    if (index === -1) {
        throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
    }

    // Moving it would put it behind the tasks with the same priority.
    if (this.#queue[index]!.priority === priority) {
        return;
    }

    const [item] = this.#queue.splice(index, 1);
    this.enqueue(item!.run, {priority, id});
}
```

Note: `PriorityQueue.setPriority` does NOT validate finite priority; it accepts `Infinity`/`-Infinity`. The finite check exists only in `PQueue.setPriority`.

`remove` overloads lines 107-118, `dequeue` lines 120-122, `filter` lines 124-135 (only filters by `priority`, matching `sizeBy`), `get size` lines 137-139.

4. `PQueue` waiting vs running metadata

`source/index.ts`:

```ts
// lines 81-90
type WaitingTask = {
	id?: string;
	// Kept here, because `.setPriority()` changes it while the task waits, and `runningTasks` reports it.
	priority?: number;
	promise?: Promise<unknown>;
	reject?: (reason: unknown) => void;
	stopListeningForAbort?: () => void;
	isCleared?: boolean;
};
```

```ts
// line 143
readonly #runningTasks = new Set<{
	id: string;
	priority: number;
	startTime: number;
	timeout: number | undefined;
}>();
// line 154
readonly #waitingTasks = new Set<WaitingTask>();
```

Lifecycle facts, all in `source/index.ts`:

- `#add` (lines 613-768): ids default to incrementing BigInt-as-string, `const id = options.id ?? (this.#idAssigner++).toString();` (line 615). Waiting priority defaults to 0: `waitingTask.priority = options.priority ?? 0;` (line 632). It enqueues the run then adds to `#waitingTasks` (lines 736-738).
- `#add` priority validation (lines 626-629) allows `Infinity`, rejects `NaN`/non-numbers:

```ts
if (options.priority !== undefined && (typeof options.priority !== 'number' || Number.isNaN(options.priority))) {
    throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a number, got \`${String(options.priority)}\` (${typeof options.priority})`);
}
```

- When a task starts (`run` closure, lines 641-733): line 644 `this.#waitingTasks.delete(waitingTask);`; lines 656-662 create `runningTask` with `priority: waitingTask.priority!` and add it to `#runningTasks`.
